    "problems": [
      {
        "category": "partial_closeout_defers_review_debt",
        "summary": "Eighteen Fortran build-input files sat in the tree unreviewed for a full day because 1DMIX-054 closed partial without a sealed documentation report, and the pinned baseline then made an unrelated issue carry them. They received their first review only as a side effect of closing 1DMIX-059.",
        "evidence": "Of this closeout's 33 judgment targets, 18 were 1DMIX-054's two *_code_validation/ directories. loop_history shows 1DMIX-054 partial with no maintenance.documentation reference, which is also why ESX 1.4.0 carried_forward attribution could not cover them: only completed closeouts confer it. Richard's review of those 18 was substantive rather than a rubber stamp -- he verified the instrumented sources are real symlinks byte-identical to the canonical copies, SIZE.h grid literals match each experiment, and each packages.conf change is exactly one documented line.",
        "minutes_lost": 12
      },
      {
        "category": "bounded_first_step_paid_off",
        "summary": "Recorded because the schema has no success channel: aiming the reviewer at the two genuinely distinct concerns, rather than at the change in general, is what made a 33-target closeout worth its cost. The flag-choice judgment was tested against MITgcm source and the unreviewed scaffolding was actually inspected, both because the question named them separately and said not to skim the second.",
        "evidence": "Richard tested the exclude_doublediff decision against kpp_calc.F:533-545 and all 8 captured inputs rather than accepting the stated reasoning, confirming the alternative would have rejected every existing configuration. He read ri_iwmix end to end plus a repo-wide grep to confirm the guard's premise, and broke the guard in a scratch copy to confirm the witness fails. One Bob turn at $0.97 and one Richard turn at $4.67 closed the issue with no correction round.",
        "minutes_lost": 0
      }
    ],
    "solutions": [
      {
        "problem": 0,
        "action": "filed",
        "issue": "TEAM-BASELINE-PIN-001",
        "reason": "That issue owns the pinned-baseline consequence, and this occurrence sharpens it: the cost is not only re-judging accepted work, which ESX 1.4.0 now attributes, but inheriting genuinely unreviewed work from a partial closeout, which carried_forward deliberately cannot cover."
      },
      {
        "problem": 1,
        "action": "filed",
        "issue": "TEAM-ORIENTATION-RESEAL-001",
        "reason": "Recorded under the brief-generation owner as the positive counterpart to its failures: naming the distinct concerns in the reviewer's question, and saying explicitly not to skim the second, is what produced a substantive review of both."
      }
    ],
    "carry_forward": [
      "Seal a documentation report even when an iteration closes partial, if its own work landed: an unsealed partial leaves that work unreviewed, ineligible for carried-forward attribution, and inherited by whichever unrelated issue closes next.",
      "When a closeout inherits another issue's unreviewed work, name it in the reviewer's question and say not to skim it. Eighteen inherited build-input files got a real first review that way rather than a rubber stamp."
    ],
    "no_problem_reason": ""
